new information about the deadly dive  boat fire in California raises concerns  about what are the crew broke any laws  the wreckage of the Concepcion was  pulled from the water near Santa Barbara  yesterday Jonathan Vigliotti has the new  details on the day crews lifted the  doomed dive boat near Santa Cruz Island  federal investigators raised new  concerns about what led to the fire that  sank it I'm pleased to report that the  salvage operations have so far been  successful an NTSB report says all six  crew members on the boat were asleep  when the fire broke out in the early  morning of September second  investigators say one crew member was  required to be awake and on Patrol five  crew members jumped into the water and  survived but 34 people were trapped  below deck all of them died this was a  very challenging case all round the  location the relatively remote location  22 miles or so away from the coastline  the charred remains of the Concepcion  will now be carefully examined  investigators are considering whether  lithium-ion batteries used to power  phones and cameras overheated and caused  the fire the tragedy prompted the US  Coast Guard to reissue its emergency  water safety guidelines which include  limiting the unsupervised charging of  lithium-ion batteries and power strips  for CBS this morning  I'm Jonathan Vigliotti
 

